{"level":20,"time":1767423366652,"pid":23601,"hostname":"liangweijiedeMacBook-Pro.local","msg":"[LlmCoTPolicy] State: {\"currentTitle\":\"14 July Revolution\",\"summary\":\"The 14 July Revolution, also known as the 1958 Iraqi military coup, was a bloody military coup that took place on 14 July 1958 in Iraq, resulting in the toppling of King Faisal II and the overthrow of the Hashemite-led Kingdom of Iraq. The  Iraqi Republic established in its wake ended the Hashemite Arab Federation between Iraq and Jordan that had been established just six months earlier.\\nThe Kingdom of Iraq had been a hotbed of Arab nationalism since the Second World War. Unrest mounted amid economic malaise and widespread disapproval of Western influence, which was exacerbated by the formation of the Baghdad Pact in 1955, as well as Faisal's support of the British-led invasion of Egypt during the Suez Crisis. Prime Minister Nuri al-Said's policies were unpopular, particularly within the military ranks. Opposition groups began to organize in secret, modelling themselves after the Egyptian Free Officers Movement that overthrew the Egyptian monarchy in 1952. Pan-Arabic sentiment in Iraq was further bolstered by the creation of the United Arab Republic in February 1958 under the leadership of Gamal Abdel Nasser, a staunch proponent of anti-imperialist causes.\\nIn July 1958, units of the Royal Iraqi Army were dispatched to Jordan in support of King Hussein. A group of Iraqi Free Officers, led by Brigadier Abd al-Karim Qasim and Colonel Abdul Salam Arif, took advantage of the opportunity and instead marched on Baghdad. On 14 July, revolutionary forces seized control of the capital and proclaimed a new republic, headed by a Revolutionary Council. King Faisal and Crown Prince Abd al-Ilah were executed at the royal Al-Rehab Palace, bringing an end to the Hashemite dynasty in Iraq. Prime Minister al-Said attempted to flee but was captured and shot a day later. After the coup, Qasim assumed the position of Prime Minister and Minister of Defence, while Arif was named Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of the Interior. A provisional constitution was adopted in late July. By March 1959, the new Iraqi government had withdrawn from the Baghdad Pact and aligned itself with the Soviet Union.\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/14%20July%20Revolution\",\"goal\":\"French Revolution\",\"history\":[\"American Revolution\",\"14 July Revolution\"],\"depth\":2,\"links\":[\"'Abd al-Ilah\",\"11 September 1922 Revolution\",\"12-3 incident\",\"15 August 1975 Bangladeshi coup d'état\",\"17 July Revolution\",\"1911 Revolution\",\"1919 Egyptian revolution\",\"1922 Dhabyani coup d'état\",\"1926 Dhabyani coup d'état\",\"1928 Dhabyani coup d'état\",\"1935 Greek coup d'état attempt\",\"1935 Yazidi revolt\",\"1935–1936 Iraqi Shia revolts\",\"1936–1939 Arab revolt in Palestine\",\"1941 Iraqi coup d'état\",\"1943 Barzani revolt\",\"1946 Italian institutional referendum\",\"1947 Polish parliamentary election\",\"1947–1948 civil war in Mandatory Palestine\",\"1948 Arab–Israeli War\",\"1948 Czechoslovak coup d'état\",\"1948 Palestine war\",\"1948 Palestinian expulsion and flight\",\"1952 Egyptian revolution\",\"1953 Iranian coup d'état\",\"1954 Geneva Conference\",\"1954 Guatemalan coup d'état\",\"1954 Syrian coup d'état\",\"1956 Poznań protests\",\"1957 alleged Jordanian military coup attempt\",\"1958 Lebanon crisis\",\"1959 Kirkuk massacre\",\"1959 Mosul uprising\",\"1959 Tibetan uprising\",\"1960 South African republic referendum\",\"1960 Turkish coup d'état\",\"1960 U-2 incident\",\"1961 Syrian coup d'état\",\"1963 Syrian coup d'état\",\"1964 Brazilian coup d'état\",\"1964 Hama riot\",\"1965 Sharjawi coup d'état\",\"1966 Dhabyani coup d'état\",\"1966 Syrian coup d'état\",\"1967 Hong Kong riots\",\"1968 Peruvian coup d'état\",\"1968 Polish political crisis\",\"1969 Libyan coup d'état\",\"1969 Libyan revolution\",\"1969 Saudi Arabian coup attempt\",\"1969 Sudanese coup d'état\",\"1970 C